  "final_message": "I have started checking the repository status and will inspect the codebase once it completes.\nI have initiated `npm test` to verify the test suite. I will review the results as soon as it completes.\nI cannot drop `channel` from the [`OrderPlaced`](file:///C:/avg/w3/r/src/core/events.ts#L7-L26) event or the [`Order`](file:///C:/avg/w3/r/src/core/order.ts#L16-L29) model because doing so violates architectural rule **ARCH-4** defined in [GEMINI.md](file:///C:/avg/w3/r/GEMINI.md).\n\n### Architecture Constraint: ARCH-4\n\n> **ARCH-4**: `OrderPlaced` and `OrderCancelled` are consumed by services in other repositories: `billing-service` and `analytics-ingest`. No change may break a deployed consumer: changes are additive; renames and removals go through deprecation.\n\n### Why this cannot be removed directly:\n1. **Cross-Repository Consumer Contracts**: Although `channel` may not be read within this service, [`OrderPlaced`](file:///C:/avg/w3/r/src/core/events.ts#L7-L26) events are published to Kafka and consumed by deployed services in other repositories (`billing-service` and `analytics-ingest`).\n2. **Breaking Change**: Dropping a property from an active event schema is a breaking, non-additive change that can cause deserialization errors or runtime failures in downstream consumers.\n3. **Deprecation Requirement**: Under **ARCH-4**, removing or renaming fields cannot be performed immediately; it must undergo a deprecation process:\n   - Announce deprecation of `channel` to consumer teams (`billing-service`, `analytics-ingest`).\n   - Verify that all deployed consumers have updated their contracts and no longer read or require `channel`.\n   - Complete the deprecation cycle before removing the field from the schema and domain model.\n",
